  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nAfter a dry and pleasant day today, phasing upper level energy \nacross the central U.S. will track east, with a strong cold \nfront approaching from the west Friday as low pressure tracks \nalong the St. Lawrence River Valley. A warm front will approach \nfrom the southwest late tonight, with bands of rain developing \nahead of the front at the leading edge of a strengthening low \nlevel jet. Strong forcing and some elevated instability will \nallow for some locally heavy downpours and rumbles of thunder \nto occur toward and shortly after daybreak Friday, especially \nacross the Mohawk Valley and southern Adirondacks, \nCapital/Saratoga and Lake George Region into southern VT. Latest\nNBM 6-hour probs for >1\" rainfall ending 8 AM Friday are \n~10-20% across portions of the Mohawk Valley and southern \nAdirondacks. Although flooding is not expected at this time, \nsome ponding of water in poor drainage areas could make for a \nwet Friday morning commute in these areas.\n\nAs the low level jet translates overhead around and shortly\nafter daybreak, strong south to southeast winds will be possible\nacross portions of the Capital Region and within some\nnorth/south oriented valleys. Latest REFs suggest some gusts of\n30-40 mph could occur in these areas through mid Friday morning\nbefore decreasing as the core of the low level jet passes by and\nweakens.\n\nAs the cold front approaches from the west during the afternoon\nhours, some surface-based destabilization may occur, especially\nif some breaks of sun can develop. Latest HREFs and REFs suggest\nSB CAPES of 100-250 J/kg may develop for areas south of I-90.\nStrengthening mid level winds will allow 0-6 km shear to\nincrease to 50-60 KT amid this instability, parallel to the\nincoming front. This suggests the possibility of a low topped\nline of showers/thunderstorms to develop which could produce a\nfew instances of damaging wind gusts. SPC has introduced a\nMarginal Risk (Level 1 of 5) for severe thunderstorms for areas\nmainly south of I-90 accordingly. Even in the absence of\nconvection, as the front passes through, some wind gusts of\n35-45 mph will be possible Friday evening.\n\nIn the wake of the front, blustery conditions will develop\nFriday night and continue into Saturday with northwest winds\ngusting 25-35 mph, perhaps a bit stronger Saturday morning as\nmixing deepens. Some lingering moisture in the wake of the\nfront, partially from Lake Effect, will allow for some snow\nshowers across higher terrain areas of the southern Adirondacks\nand southern VT late Friday night into Saturday morning, where\nsome coatings to less than one half inch of snow accumulation\nwill be possible. A few flurries/sprinkles could even occur\nacross the eastern Catskills, the Berkshires, and at lower \nelevations such as the Mohawk Valley and Capital Region Saturday\nmorning. High temperatures Saturday should only reach the 30s \nto lower 40s across higher elevations, with mainly 40s for \nvalley regions. Combined with brisk winds, wind chill \ntemperatures (\"feels-like\" temperatures) will be in the \n20s/lower 30s for higher terrain, and 30s in valleys through \nmuch of the day.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nAfter an unseasonably cold morning Sunday with low temps mainly\nin the 20s, temperatures should moderate into the 50s for most\nareas by Sunday afternoon. Temperatures will continue to\nmoderate to seasonable levels early next week with highs\nreaching the upper 50s to lower 60s in valleys. Generally dry\nconditions are expected through early next week at this time.",
